perfcached (0.4.0~rc33-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A dump that crosses a table resize (an operator's resize, or the
    automatic shrink after mass deletes) is refused and redone instead of
    repeating records or silently losing them: every dump reply names its
    table, and perfdump dumps a swapped collection again.  Measured before:
    a shrink between two chunks lost 2,200 of 3,000 records.
  * /metrics gains perfcached_rdb_last_duration_seconds, _saves_total,
    _last_success_age_seconds and _in_progress, so the snapshot alert
    documented in rc32 can be written; the README gives the rule.
  * save = off no longer claims to disable snapshots: the WAL still asks
    for them when its free segments run short.
  * contrib/grafana/perfcached.json: a dashboard over every series.

 -- Yury Kirsanov <yury@kfi.net.au>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 21:40:00 +1000

perfcached (0.4.0~rc32-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The WAL asks for a snapshot early enough under heavy writes: a new
    trigger compares the time left until the ring is full, at the current
    fill rate, with twice the last snapshot's duration.  On an 8 MB ring
    against a ~4 s snapshot the old quarter-free trigger refused 10,020 of
    18,000 writes; the new one refused none.
  * The triggers now run from the first segment change, not only after the
    ring's first wrap - a fresh node with save = off could overrun.
  * stats.wal gains fill_mb_s and full_in_s; /metrics the matching gauges.
  * save = 0 N is refused with the reason.
  * sweepclocktest and tombstonetest wait for a witness (rc30 and rc31
    were red on them).

 -- Yury Kirsanov <yury@kfi.net.au>  Fri, 25 Sep 2026 20:40:00 +1000

perfcached (0.4.0~rc31-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A clean stop (SIGTERM) now takes a snapshot on the way down, so a node
    stopped on purpose comes back complete on its own: a three-node eager
    fleet stopped cleanly, one node restarted alone, now serves 300 of 300
    keys where it served 100.  A kill -9 recovers exactly as before.
  * New privileged verb fleetstop: stops every node in the fleet that way
    and reports which went - perfcli -E <enable secret> fleetstop.
  * Status page: the hit-rate bars line up whatever their caption says.

 -- Yury Kirsanov <yury@kfi.net.au>  Thu, 25 Sep 2026 19:45:00 +1000

perfcached (0.4.0~rc30-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A whole-fleet outage no longer makes an eager fleet delete its own
    data.  The reconcile pass, which drops a replayed key no peer still
    has, now runs only if some live peer was up throughout this node's
    absence (booted at least 30 s before it went down).  A fleet that
    came back together - at once, one by one, or staggered - has no such
    witness, skips the pass and deletes nothing.  Measured before the
    fix: 600 of 900 keys lost after a simultaneous restart.
  * New: stats.cluster.reconcile_witness and reconcile_skipped, and
    perfcached_cluster_reconcile_skipped.  No wire change.

 -- Yury Kirsanov <yury@kfi.net.au>  Thu, 25 Sep 2026 19:10:00 +1000

