 -- Yury Kirsanov <yury@kfi.net.au>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 13:51:32 +1000

perfcached (0.3.0~rc32-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The table now keeps up with a write burst.  The splitter's budget was
    a flat 128 splits per maintenance tick whatever the deficit, so a
    600,000-record fill left a table at load factor 102 needing about
    nineteen minutes of ticking to reach its target of 4, with everything
    that would not fit six to a bucket going to the overflow leg.  The
    budget is now bounded by time rather than by a count: measured 2.5x to
    4.3x the write rate over four paired runs, load factor 102-109 down to
    4.2-5.4.
  * make check-asan-findings scans a sanitizer log with the broken-locks
    negative control excluded, since that control always produces a
    finding and a plain grep therefore always matches.
  * No wire, config or metric change; interoperates with rc31.

 -- Yury Kirsanov <yury@kfi.net.au>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 10:32:16 +1000

perfcached (0.3.0~rc31-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The arena's ceiling now covers the index a table is built from, not
    only the records: a region carve fell through to shm_malloc with no
    test at all, so one create could put a node 21% past its arena_mb and
    an ordinary table split could do the same.  A whole index prices
    itself and tests the ceiling before its first carve.
  * A table larger than the segment directory is refused rather than
    written past, and [collection] buckets_log2 takes 4..24 as the verbs
    already did.
  * Replica-lag figures on the cluster plane - applied per second, the
    receive queue against its buffer, per-socket drops - so a node that
    cannot keep up with inbound copies says so instead of looking
    healthy.  Plus the overflow leg and the held walk's cost per
    collection.
  * The held-bytes walk mis-timed itself, reporting about 1,271 seconds
    on half of all walks, which its own pacing then read.
  * The status page keeps five minutes of history, not an hour.

